  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4x-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4x-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 3 \\\\ u & 3 & 11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{(4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(u){|}_{3}^{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(11)-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}ln\\,(\\frac{11}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
